# Review

## Summary

The note does not reduce to one simple insight. One paragraph would have to compress three things. First, it would have to compress the urgency argument: collection goals admit notes but do not rank them, and maintenance cost grows with autonomous inflow. Second, it would have to compress five distinct valuation lenses, each with its own signal and grounding note. Third, it would have to compress four separate open problems.

Each open problem carries its own failure condition or boundary case:
- a top-level note can look orphaned while a closed cluster looks well linked;
- validity is checked more strongly than value;
- downstream signals cannot be observed at admission;
- a composite score can be Goodharted.

Merging these into one paragraph would lose the separation between the current answer and the unsettled problems, and that separation is the purpose of a standing query. The multi-section structure is justified.
